<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Na'aman River</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">Na'aman</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Na'aman is a short coastal river in northern Israel that flows into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> just south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a>. Its mouth lies beside Tel Akko, the mound of ancient Acre, which used it as a harbour in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>. Known in antiquity as the Belus, it was famous for the sand used in making glass, described by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a>.</p></div>
